---
title: "Anmatyerr-akert warrk-irrem (working with Anmatyerr people)"
date: 2026-10-30T08:46:12+10:00
draft: true
---

A few weeks ago I returned home from a four-month-long field trip to Central Australia. I was stationed there to work with Anmatyerr people (*Anmatyerr-akert* 'Anmatyerr-with', *warrk-irrem* 'working'), for some research on the sound system and grammar of their language. Anmatyerr is an Arandic (Pama-Nyungan) language spoken across a large stretch of land about two hundred kilometres north of Alice Springs. (Well, eventually I came to learn that there's not really one particular linguistic system which can be identified as "Anmatyerr", but a collection of closely related language varieties -- the linguistic boundaries between which were remarkably difficult to pin down. We'll hear about this a bit later.) I was launched right into it, really without an orientation, and I learnt much along the way. David Moore (a fellow Arandicist and wonderful fieldwork companion) suggested I lay out in text some of my experiences as a first time fieldworker. And so I'm doing just that. In this post I discuss my field trip ['preparation'](#preparation), how I [found Anmatyerr consultants](#finding-consultants), working with ['different dialects'](#different-dialects) of the language, some nightmarish [car troubles](#car-troubles), and finally the [psychosocial hazards](#psychosocial-hazards) of my various work setups.

#### Preparation {#preparation}

To start, let me say that I was grossly underprepared for the trip. Before all this I had never taken a linguistic field methods course, learnt a real language from scratch, driven a manual car, worked with Aboriginal people, or really had to appease a university's risk assessment manager before. And I would like to think I got marginally better at most of these, though not without some suffering along the way. Although we{{% sidenote "sn-we" %}}With 'we', I'm referring to myself and Robert Mailhammer, my boss at Western Sydney University who helped tremendously with all the planning and bureaucracy.{{% /sidenote %}} had planned out most of the details of my trip, we came up against some frustrating bureaucratic obstacles. The first of these ended up being the university's travel policy, which prohibits stay at private accommodation like a rental or sharehouse. Only hotels and the like. So we had to fork out for a serviced apartment, which had a range of benefits and drawbacks that I'll discuss later. Evidently, this policy was not designed with fieldwork in mind -- what would I do, for instance, if I were carrying out my work in a tiny village situated in the Papuan Highlands? Just not go, apparently. To nobody's surprise, the CFO was not willing to revise the policy for me, and so I ended up in commercial accommodation.

While we were working this all out, we ran into the concomitant issue that is risk assessment. We (or, I should say, **I**) had to prepare a very long document outlining how I will control for the potential risks of fieldwork in Central Australia. You know, all the usual stuff, like snake bites, heatstroke, medical care, cultural awareness, access to psychological care, emergency contact routines, and so on. In a back-and-forth effort with the risk assessment officer, we were made aware of all the hidden risks which would totally compromise my trip, such as computer screen angles and chair ergonomics. It turned out these last two were crucial considerations which saved my life. One of the strongly-emphasised risks was the psychosocial hazard of doing work in a 'remote' location privated{{% sidenote "sn-privated" %}}I've decided that this should be a verb-less gerundive adjective, [<span class="ipa">pɹɪˈvæɪtəd</span>], with a meaning like the etymologically related *deprived*.{{% /sidenote %}} of my friends and family.

Removing the automatic transmission restriction from my probationary license ended up being the first, and most agonising, hurdle relating to this trip. I was expecting to leave for Alice Springs as soon as I was put on my fixed term contract, which was processed on the twenty-second of May. My manual driving test was scheduled for the twenty-fifth, and so in principle I could leave straight after that. I wanted to be in Central Australia as soon as possible, to maximise my time there. After months of lessons, the twenty-fifth rolls around and with the nerves absolutely wrecking me, I fail the drive test about three minutes in. Changed lanes poorly. As we all know, these things have to be booked a long time in advance, sometimes up to six weeks. When we arrive back at VicRoads we find out I can take the test again two days later, but in Mitcham, on roads with which I am unfamiliar. This was similarly catastrophic, and I fail a second time, although nearing the end of the test. I felt totally defeated by this point, comforted only slightly by the fact that things were moving sluggishly in terms of travel and budget approval for the trip, meaning it wasn't certain when I'd leave. We rebooked the test for the week after, on the second of June, where I passed with a great score. Thankfully, and finally. I'm just not a great driver.

That afternoon, I went back-and-forth with WSU over the risk assessment, fixing things up so it could be approved ASAP. Eventually I had to agree to contact three nominated individuals once a day so that they could verify I was still living. The next morning, the third of June, I woke up and again was met with more demands about the risk assessment report, claiming I hadn't considered a number of things but that if I addressed them, we could submit the entire travel claim and have it approved by the Dean by noon, the cutoff. The Dean, the CFO, and the Provost of the university all had to sign off on the trip because it was one of the most expensive travel claims in WSU history, apparently, so scale of the budget was unusually large. I get a call at 11:57, from an unknown number: "Hi Geordie, this is the WSU travel team. We only have three minutes so we'll dispense with the formalities. If you give us verbal confirmation that you're happy to fly to Alice Springs at 08:25 tomorrow morning, we'll push this report through to the Dean so she can approve it right now," I hear. "Yes!", I eject. And so it was all done, with a minute to spare. I did in fact leave the very next morning. I can tell you now that I'll never be leaving it that late again.



#### Finding consultants the hard way {#finding-consultants}

Perhaps I didn't have the usual 21<sup>st</sup> century fieldwork experience, in that there were no Anmatyerr people lined up for me to meet. All I was told was that there are Anmatyerr people living in Alice Springs with whom I could work. I didn't know their names, addresses, or anything about them. It seems that many people nowadays at least go to the fieldsite for a short trip, a couple of weeks perhaps, to introduce themselves to the community and make connections to enable a future trip. I didn't have this opportunity, and so I went straight there without knowing anybody. My first attempts at locating Anmatyerr speakers was largely a failure. I assumed the commonest Aboriginal language spoken in Alice Springs would be Mparntwe Arrernte, and so I learned some basic Arrernte phrases and grammar from the [learner's guide](https://theswissbay.ch/pdf/Books/Linguistics/Mega%20linguistics%20pack/Australian/Arrernte%2C%20A%20Learner%27s%20Guide%20to%20Eastern%20and%20Central%20%28Green%29.pdf). Just simple things like *werte!* 'what's up!', *unte Anmatyerre angkeme?* 'do you speak Anmatyerr?', *nthenhe the Anmatyerreke untheme?* 'where can I look for Anmatyerr (people)?', and so on. Of course, people just ignored me when I tried these, and in hindsight I realise this was not the best method for finding consultants. Later on in my trip I had no issue chatting up strangers in Anmatyerr, but as a starting point this was not the way to go.

