So I asked David Moore if he knew any speakers, and he put me in touch with Paul Traeger, a pastor at the Finke River Mission Lutheran Church of Australia, who might be able to introduce me to some Anmatyerr people from the church. And so I got in contact, and a few days later went over to the mission block and had a chat with him. The church is located on Strehlow Street, The Gap, which is the same street I was staying on and the street on which I believe the great Ken Hale's house used to be.{{% sidenote "sn-hale-house" %}}Although, I'm not entirely sure and nobody has confirmed this. But in [Sally Hale's](https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/server/api/core/bitstreams/1eeb2b99-093e-4b3a-80de-af4564789f6e/content) and [Jenny Green's](https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/server/api/core/bitstreams/2a206fd3-516b-438a-8b78-7c849fb9920b/content) chapters in *Forty years on: Ken Hale and Australian languages*, it is mentioned at various points that Ken and Sally lived by the Todd river, across the street from (or next to?) the "Lutheran mission (block)". The best I can work out is that it used to be on the corner of [Strehlow Street and South Terrace](https://maps.app.goo.gl/pEujy2ee3P5Ddupo7), which coincidentally was where I was staying!{{% /sidenote %}} I walked into the mission block and met with Paul, and we had a bit of a chat about my work and why I was there. He suggested that I might be able to work with a man named Ryan Nelson, or Kwetyay [<span class="ipa">ˈkʊcɛɪ</span>], as I would come to know him by. In need of Anmatyerr teachers, I hastily agreed. "Great", Paul replied, "let me call him now." He rings up Ryan and, in fluent Luritja, explains that there's a whitefella here at the church who wants to record Anmatyerr language. Hanging up the phone, he tells me, "Ryan's coming now, he'll be here in an hour and you can start recording then." Gulp. I hadn't prepared any elicitation material. And I still needed to go and get some cash to pay Ryan.

I went out for an hour to get lunch and some money, and upon my return Ryan was sitting there in the Finke office in which I had met Paul. I pulled out my Zoom H5 and my notebook, not knowing what would come next. "This is Geordie Kidd, I'm here on Strehlow Street on the ninth of June, 2026 with Ryan Nelson and Paul Traeger, and we're doing some Anmatyerr elicitation." Shit... what do I say next? "Okay, uh, maybe you can tell me the word for *man*," I uttered sheepishly. "Rtwang [<span class="ipa">ˈʈwɒŋ</span>]," Kwetyay replied, and I scrawled down what I heard. "How would I say, *I saw the man*," I continued. "Thang arek artwang [<span class="ipa">t̪ɐŋɐˈɻəkɐˌʈwɒŋ</span>]," he said. And so we went on, for about two hours. I tried to elicit basic tenses, some vocab, some cultural knowledge, greetings and goodbyes -- anything that I could think of, really.

I was totally rubbish at hearing the sounds of this language at the beginning. But since I thought I had to write everything down, the sessions progressed slowly and I was more focused on live transcription than really hearing what was going on. I should have done what Ken Hale used to do, which was elicit a batch of sentences in a straightforward paradigm, and transcribe them all afterwards. It's certainly more efficient that way. Oh well.{{% sidenote "sn-hale" %}}The first time I heard a recording of Hale's fieldwork I felt like quitting. He introduces the Kaytetye speaker in his beautiful, thick Chicago accent, and then in the background you hear somebody providing Arrernte sentences in order to elicit the direct Kaytetye translations. It took me over 30 seconds to realise it was Hale himself, and not a native Arrernte speaker, producing these sentences.{{% /sidenote %}}

Ryan quickly became my most tireless teacher, and (eventually) a very close friend. His breadth of knowledge of Anmatyerr language, culture, people, land, and society was always stimulating, and his unconditional generosity in sharing these with me -- from the outset -- is something I will never cease to be grateful for. He sat with me on more occasions than I can count, patiently guiding me through the minutiae of Anmatyerr segmental phonology,{{% sidenote %}}For the linguists reading: Ryan was one of those teachers who would make great efforts to provide phonetic descriptions of words to me, on top of producing them. To take two examples, I had a lot of trouble distinguishing [j] and [<span class="ipa">ʎ</span>] word-initially (these can be surprisingly hard to distinguish in the absence of attack cues!), as well as perceiving contrastive vowel-length medially in some complex forms. For the palatals, I once kept interpreting [<span class="ipa">ʎɪl̪əm</span>] 'sing to oneself' as [<span class="ipa">jɪl̪əm</span>]. And we all know that feeling of repeating the same word after the speaker a dozen times thinking we've gotten it right, only to eventually recognise their frustration. Ryan thoughtfully put an end to this by making a boomerang-throwing gesture and hyper-articulating the word [<span class="ipa">ʎɐŋ</span>] 'boomerang', which I could recognise. Fantastic. For the long vowels, there is a contrast in some associated motion forms between [<span class="ipa">...ɪcɐl̪əm</span>] 'do <...> after arriving' and [<span class="ipa">...ɪcɐːl̪əm</span>] 'arrive with the intention of doing <...>'. As Ryan and others pointed out, "for the second one you gotta drag im just a little bit longer." How could I have ended up with better teachers?{{% /sidenote %}} various kinds of grammatical constructions, differences between how older and younger Anmatyerr people talk, and much more. He introduced me to most of the Anmatyerr people I now know, and was always eager to explain to them that he was teaching me the language. People were very excited upon hearing this, and always encouraged it. Ryan, and indeed his close friends with whom I also worked, are all Lutherans and have worked with [David Strickland](/blog/2026/david-strickland) on bible translation. This meant they were very switched on to the subtle meanings of constructions, and how to explain these meanings. "If somebody is dancing for a long time, like right here, you can say <nobr>*rntwep-rntwem*</nobr>. But if somebody is dancing for a long time one place over there, where you can't see him, maybe they're around the corner, you say <nobr>*rntwerl-anem*</nobr>," Ryan told me once. And it turns out that this really is the semantic difference between these two types of constructions. Several other Anmatyerr men I worked with were equally good at this. Showing me particular generosity in this domain were: Nathanial Dixon Ngwarray, Savior George Pengart, Ian Nelson Penangk, Ambrose Joe Kemarr, Darren Presley Penangk, and Murray Nelson Pengart.

{{< image width=80% src=/img/me-kwetyay.jpeg cap="Me and Ryan Nelson (Kwetyay) in front of the Laramba sign." >}}

My consultants for Angka Angerrakw-iperr (Eastern Anmatyerr) I met via a mutual contact. The linguist who had come for a brief two-week trip to work on Angerrakw-iperr passed on the number of Jedda Ngwarray Purvis, with whom she had worked during that trip. I eventually got onto Jedda in July, although commencing the work with her was complicated by some vehicular difficulties, described below. I ended up recording with Jedda, as well as her sisters Judy and Maureen, and friends Rayleen Pula Price, Joy Petyarr Purvis, and Louisa Ngwarray Long, quite a bit, although I shamefully didn't do a very good job of learning Angerrakw-iperr. At least one good thing came of our work, though, which is a recording of the [50 Words list](https://50words.online/languages/Angka%20Angerrakw-iperr%20(Eastern%20Anmatyerr)). Go there to listen to how this beautiful language sounds. All the women I worked with are consummate artists; Jedda, Judy, Maureen, and Louisa are the granddaughters of Emily Kam Kngwarray, the famed Eastern Anmatyerr painter. Some of the most fun I had on my trip involved recording with these ladies.



#### Car troubles {#car-troubles} 

If you remember, part of my preparation involved getting a license to drive manual cars. Doing so enabled me to drive a 2017 Toyota Hilux, without which much of the fieldwork would have been impossible. And it was truly fun to drive. But it wasn't quite so simple. The car is owned by the University of Melbourne, and can be hired for linguistic fieldwork in Central Australia at a fairly good rate. The day after I landed in Alice Springs, Samantha Disbray dropped the car off to me after having just completed a field trip in Papunya.{{% sidenote "sn-papunya" %}}I heard a young Anmatyerr girl call this place [<span class="ipa">ˈpʊpɐɲ</span>], with no final [<span class="ipa">ə ~ ɐ</span>], despite the Luritja pronunciation universally being [<span class="ipa">ˈpʊpɐɲɐ</span>]. This confirmed to me that Anmatyerr speakers have lost final central vowels on words.{{% /sidenote %}} That was on 05 June. I was advised around that time that I needed to take the car into Alice Springs Toyota for a routine service, and on 23 June I did. I was told by Toyota I'd get it back the same day. Two days passed and I had heard nothing, so I decided to drop by the service centre to check up on the vehicle.

Toyota: *Oh, yes, well it needs new engine mounts and we had to order those parts in from Sydney. That was the cause of the noise you were hearing. They should get here on Friday, and it's likely by Monday you'll be able to pick it up. We just couldn't give it back to you, because it's not safe to drive.*

It would have been nice to hear this on the day of assessment. But no worries. The noise -- the deep vibration of the engine rumbling around in there -- I had wrongly presumed was a normal diesel engine noise. So I waited until the end of the week. I couldn't really do work with people as I had no way to pick them up. A week went by -- nothing. I called up Toyota on Monday and they told me they were still waiting on the parts. A few days go by again, and I'm getting slightly impatient. Not only because I couldn't record with people, but because I had a 4WD driving course coming up and I needed the car for that. It had been two weeks, and I'd heard nothing. This was getting to be an absurdly long time to wait for a relatively simple repair, so I visited Toyota again and spoke with someone else, who informed me that the car needed a new transmission, and that they were waiting on one to arrive from Japan before they could fit it and give it back. A new transmission! I couldn't believe my ears. I don't know what people could have been doing with it prior to me, and all I did was drive on bitumen roads in town. Oh well, I couldn't do anything before Toyota got the parts in.

Because I am still on a probationary license, I couldn't hire a commercial vehicle in Alice Springs (since these all require a full license). I had no idea how long this period of vehiclelessness would persist, but I was hoping it wouldn't be much longer. The weeks went by. Doing fieldwork became very, very difficult. Around that time, I had just gotten in contact with an Angerrakw-iperr (Eastern Anmatyerr) speaker who was keen to work. The only solution I was able to settle on was to use taxis to ferry her (and her sisters and friends) over to my house, and then back home after recording. This was very expensive, hugely inconvenient, and unreliable due to the mind-blowing unwillingness of Alice Springs taxi drivers to do any trips not involving the airport as a destination.

I'm pretty sure everyone hated this taxi setup. Even my consultants from Laramba got tired of it, some even refusing to work if this was the only means of transportation. Thankfully, some of my teachers had cars and were, to the amazement of both me and my colleagues, willing to drive to my accommodation to record with me. I still can't believe this. But it wasn't a sustainable solution while I waited for the car back. I ended up borrowing Glenn Marshall's{{% sidenote "sn-marshalls" %}}Glenn and Jane Marshall were some of the first people I met in Alice Springs, introduced to me by Samantha who gave me their phone numbers before disappearing onto the aeroplane. And so I became good friends with them. I later learned that they are like Alice Springs royalty! Everyone knows them, and they know everyone. They're wonderful and generous people who helped me out on many occasions.{{% /sidenote %}} Hilux for the 4WD course, but ultimately didn't need it as the driving component was done in pairs, and I hopped in with someone else.

At this point it had been a month without the Hilux and my frequent checking in with Toyota led to no new updates on the timeline. You can surely imagine how frustrating this situation was. Luckily for me, David Moore had just returned from an overseas trip to Iceland, and upon returning let me know that he might have a car I could borrow. It was a charmingly seasoned single cab Ford Courier, typically used to transport firewood, I was told. I couldn't take more than one passenger in it, but it did allow me to work with people a bit more efficiently. Working with Ryan became easier, since he was happy to do one-on-one work at my residence. I think that's because we'd become quite good friends by then. For the Eastern Anmatyerr ladies, we settled on the amusing arrangement of me visiting their home in the ute, calling a taxi to take them to Batchelor, me following along in the car and paying the taxi driver at the end, and doing the whole thing again at home time. But it was better than nothing, and allowed me to get around town a bit more easily. 

I used the Ford for a couple more weeks, suffering my way through the work, until one evening I received a welcome, but wholly unexpected, call from Toyota informing me that the Hilux was ready to pick up the following morning. The ecstasy I felt in the that moment could only be compared to things I shouldn't say publicly on this blog. So the next morning, excited as ever, I drove the Ford back to Dave's house for deposit, and from there he dropped me off at the service centre in town. I'm pretty sure that on the way we talked about classifying Arandic languages at the communilect level, effectively eschewing categories like Anmatyerr and Arrernte in favour of more geographic ones like *the Arandic variety spoken in Aharlper*, to give an example. I get to Toyota and they drive the car around. Switching it on, I saw what the mechanics meant by "the noise", because the car was basically silent this time around. And it felt so, so good to drive, because they had basically rebuilt from the inside! It felt like a new car. Of course, the first thing I did was drive home, pack up a change of clothes, and head out to the Harts Range Races with Jane and Glenn to camp for a night.

{{< image width=100% src=/img/harts-range.jpeg cap="From left to right: Glenn Marshall, me, Jane Marshall. Standing in front of a fire with the sunset trailing behind.">}}

#### Different dialects, or different languages? {#different-dialects}

A majority of my work was done with mob from Laramba (*Alheramp*, 'frog'), a community about two hundred kilometres north-west of Alice Springs. 

I spent a majority of my time working with men from Laramba (*Alheramp* 'frog'), a community about two hundred kilometres north-west of Alice Springs. 

#### Psychosocial hazards {#psychosocial-hazards}

One of the major concerns flagged in the risk assessment report was that the working environment could have adverse psychosocial effects, such as inducing depression, stress, and feelings of isolation. While stress is inevitable no matter how good the setup, the others were in my view totally mitigable by relaxing the travel policy. Because of the ban on private accommodation, such as a sharehouse, I was forced to live alone in a studio apartment for four months. The work was already quite isolating, and I was living in a new place with few friends, so being confined to my own company didn't help at all. I would argue in fact that the psychosocial effects of this situation are more severe than the potential (and frankly dubious) 'risks' associated with private accommodation. I'll be making my case to the university that this needs to be accounted for in the travel policy.


